# ~/.mesh/study-fields.list — the study channel's field registry (node-local, gitignored)
# Copy to ~/.mesh/study-fields.list. mesh-study rotates ONE field per run (23 */4) and stamps a
# brief to ~/.mesh/study.log; mesh-study-bridge (41 */4) turns the newest brief into a queued
# experiment. Without this file BOTH lanes are dead — that is why it ships as an example
# (chat-review/study-lane-dead-since-migration-reports-exit-0, 2026-07-15: the registry was pure
# runtime state, lived on IdeaPad, and did not travel in the 07-14 migration).
#
# FORMAT:  field | source-url
#   source-url is an HN Algolia endpoint. Use search_by_date, NOT search (relevance) — a 3-4-word
#   phrase on the relevance endpoint freezes onto the same evergreen top-points page forever
#   (finding-study-lane-hollow-20260705.md). mesh-study appends the recency window itself
#   (numericFilters=created_at_i>cutoff, MESH_STUDY_WINDOW_DAYS, default 30) unless the url
#   already carries one — so never bake a cutoff in here; it would go stale.
#
# THE FIELDS ARE NOT INVENTED HERE. They are the table in docs/eternity-and-its-fields.md — the
# disciplines this mesh is assembled from. Each row below cites the doc row it serves.
#
# QUERIES ARE READ, NOT COUNTED. The inline number below is what a query RETURNED (page 1), and it
# is NOT a quality score — it is not even a measurement of the field. Two ways it lies, both caught
# on 2026-07-15 (chat-review/study-registry-validates-on-hit-count-which-anticorrelates-with-quality):
#
#   1. THE COUNT IS A CAP. Every "# 20 hits" row was hitsPerPage=20, not twenty matches: bare
#      query=Raft reports nbHits=503, query=CRDT nbHits=219. A row annotated 20 is a row that hit
#      the ceiling, i.e. a row about which the annotation says nothing at all.
#   2. THE COUNT ANTICORRELATES WITH ABOUTNESS. Algolia applies typo tolerance + last-word prefix
#      matching, so a SHORT SINGLE-WORD query bleeds: `Raft` matched Drafts/crafts and returned an
#      Egyptian Afterlife Walkthrough, a Hong Kong bookshop raid and a Rust build cache — 0/20
#      on-field, while scoring the registry's joint-highest count. The bridge then bought a paid
#      mind-turn implementing Rust CI for a "Raft / consensus" brief (study-bridge.log 20:41:01Z).
#      The two rows the old count ranked richest were the two that had dissolved.
#
# So the fix is NOT "count >= 1", and it is not blanket-quoting either — measured here, both halves:
#   - `&typoTolerance=false` disarms the bleed (Raft 503 -> 3, CRDT 219 -> 10) and leaves honest rows
#     untouched (Erlang 5->5, self-healing 7->7, microkernel 6->6, distributed systems 23->22) — but
#     it is NOT free to apply globally: it kills `self-replication` 5 -> 0, whose five hits are all
#     genuinely von Neumann probes and reach the row only BECAUSE typo tolerance stems
#     replication/replicating. The right transform differs per row, which is why it lives in the row.
#   - quoting ("CRDT") also disarms the bleed but drops the PLURAL: 6 hits vs typoTolerance's 10,
#     losing "Agents Attacking Documents with CRDTs" and "Garbage-collecting Byzantine,
#     open-membership CRDTs" — two of the most on-field items in the whole registry.
#   - and quoting cannot certify: `query="Raft"` -> exactly 1 hit, raft.build/resources/blog, an
#     AI-agent startup. 100% on-topic BY WORD, 0% BY FIELD. A gate keyed on the quoted count would
#     have handed that to the bridge with a certificate. Word presence is not aboutness; only a
#     reader settles it.
#
# RE-MEASURE BY READING:  mesh-study --audit   (drives every row's real url, prints the titles,
# prints no verdict). Then annotate the row with what you READ — "n on-field of m read", not a count.
# A separate trap, and still true: the field NAMES
# from the doc mostly return ZERO — "Erlang OTP supervision" 0, "anti-entropy" 0, "autopoiesis" 0,
# "evolutionary computation" 0, "supervision tree" 0, "gossip protocol" 0. Transcribing the doc's
# vocabulary literally yields a registry that EXISTS and still never produces: every run hits the
# honest "(EMPTY — 0 fresh hits)" branch, and a present-but-hollow registry is a worse lie than an
# absent one, because it looks planted. Broaden to the term the discourse actually uses ("Erlang",
# "artificial life", "write-ahead log"), and RE-READ before adding a row — `mesh-study --audit`
# once the row is in, or by hand first, printing TITLES rather than a length:
#
#   cutoff=$(( $(date +%s) - 30*86400 ))
#   curl -s "https://hn.algolia.com/api/v1/search_by_date?query=YOUR_TERM&tags=story&numericFilters=created_at_i%3E${cutoff}" \
#     | python3 -c 'import sys,json; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(d["nbHits"]); [print("-",h.get("title")) for h in d["hits"][:8]]'
#
# A 0 here is not fatal (mesh-study marks EMPTY honestly and rotates on) — and a field whose honest
# state is EMPTY is worth more than one propped up by a query that matches its letters. But a
# registry of mostly zeroes is a dead lane wearing a green face. Prune rows that go permanently dry.

# --- no center: distributed systems; stigmergy / swarm  (doc row 1; gap = CRDT-style merge) ---
# typoTolerance=false: bare `CRDT` bled to nbHits=219 of pure firehose (Ty fork, ls alternative,
# critical minerals, QR-Swastika-Avoider — 0/8 on-field). Disarmed: 10 hits, 4+ genuinely CRDT (Opbox
# disk sync, Loomabase column-level CRDT x2, "Agents Attacking Documents with CRDTs", Byzantine
# open-membership CRDT GC). Doc row 1's named gap is not a thin field — fuzz was hiding it.
CRDT / conflict-free merge      | https://hn.algolia.com/api/v1/search_by_date?query=CRDT&tags=story&typoTolerance=false  # read 2026-07-15: 4+ on-field of 10
distributed systems             | https://hn.algolia.com/api/v1/search_by_date?query=distributed%20systems&tags=story  # 20

# --- self-healing supervision: autonomic computing; Erlang/OTP; cybernetics  (doc row 2) ---
Erlang / OTP supervision        | https://hn.algolia.com/api/v1/search_by_date?query=Erlang&tags=story                 # 5  ("OTP supervision"=0, "supervision tree"=0)
self-healing systems            | https://hn.algolia.com/api/v1/search_by_date?query=self-healing&tags=story           # 7

# --- fungible mind / failover: fault-tolerant computing; Raft/Paxos  (doc row 3) ---
# RAFT IS UNUSABLE AS A QUERY AT ANY TRANSFORM — read, not counted, 2026-07-15:
#   bare -> nbHits=503, 0/20 on-field (typo-matches Drafts/crafts; this is the row that bought a paid
#           mind-turn of Rust-CI work off a "Raft / consensus" brief).
#   typoTolerance=false -> 3: a naval-combat roguelike (Raftig), raft.build's blog, a git tool.
#   "Raft" quoted -> 1: raft.build/resources/blog — an AI-agent startup. On-topic by word, not field.
# The word is too short and too owned. Paxos carries doc row 3 honestly instead: 2 hits, both real
# (Lamport's Paxos Made Simple, OceanBase Multi-Paxos failover). Thin and true beats loud and false.
Raft / Paxos consensus          | https://hn.algolia.com/api/v1/search_by_date?query=Paxos&tags=story&typoTolerance=false # read 2026-07-15: 2 on-field of 2
fault tolerance                 | https://hn.algolia.com/api/v1/search_by_date?query=fault%20tolerance&tags=story      # 5

